{"answerArea":{"calculator":true,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"Let's first find the expression for $g'(x)$ and then evaluate it at $x=27$.\n\nThe derivative of $g$ can be found using **the power rule**:\n\n$\\dfrac{d}{dx}(x^n)=n\\cdot x^{n-1}$\n\n(Remember that this applies even when $n$ is a fraction.)","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\u0026\\phantom{=}g'(x)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{d}{dx}\\left(x^{^{\\Large\\frac{4}{3}}}\\right)\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{4}{3}x^{^{\\Large\\frac{4}{3}-1}}\\qquad\\gray{\\text{The power rule}}\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=\\dfrac{4}{3}x^{^{\\Large\\frac{1}{3}}}\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"So we found that $g'(x)=\\dfrac{4}{3}x^{^{\\Large\\frac{1}{3}}}$, which can also be written as $\\dfrac{4}{3}\\sqrt[3]{x}$\n\nNow let's plug $\\blueD{x=27}$:\n\n$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac43\\sqrt[3]{\\blueD{27}}\u0026=\\dfrac43\\cdot 3\n\\\\\\\\\n\u0026=4\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"In conclusion, $g'(27)=4$.","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"Let $g(x)=x^{^{\\scriptsize\\dfrac{4}{3}}}$.\n\n$g'(27)=$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"maxError":null,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":4}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"g prime of 27","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
